          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a link between sleep and migraine?",
          "answer": "Sleep and migraine have a two-way relationship, meaning they both influence each other. Disturbed sleep can make migraine attacks more likely, and in turn, migraine attacks can disrupt sleep. Interestingly, sleep is also one of the more reliable ways an attack can end.\n\nA change in sleep pattern is a frequently reported trigger for migraines. It's not just about getting too little sleep; sleeping in can also provoke attacks. This is why \"weekend headaches\" are common, as late nights followed by long lie-ins shift sleep timing.\n\nThis information comes from the \"Sleep and migraine: regularity, and the relationship running both ways\" document in the General clinical reference and headache medicine educational summary.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
